{{head|en|verb form}}

# {{infl of|en|be||ing-form}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A living [[creature]].
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|page=126|passage=Minute grew into minute, and still there was no sign of life, nor did the curtain move; but I felt the gaze of the unknown '''being''' sinking through and through me, and filling me with a nameless terror, till the perspiration stood in beads upon my brow.}}
# The state or fact of [[existence]], [[consciousness]], or [[life]], or something in such a state.
#* '''1608-1634''', {{w|John Webster}} (and perhaps {{w|Thomas Heywood}}), ''{{w|Appius and Virginia}}''
#*: Claudius, thou / Wast follower of his fortunes in his '''being'''.
#* {{RQ:Beckett Watt|text=For the service to be considered was not the service of one servant, but of two servants, and even of three servants, and even of an infinity of servants, of whom the first could not out till the second up, nor the second up till the third in, nor the third in till the first out, nor the first out till the third in, nor the third in till the second up, nor the second up till the first out, every going, every '''being''', every coming consisting with a '''being''' and a coming, a coming and a going, a going and a '''being''', nay with all the '''beings''' and all the comings, with all the comings and all the goings, with all the goings and all the '''beings''', of all the servants that had ever served Mr. Knott, of all the servants that ever would serve Mr. Knott.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2019-11-12|title={{w|Amos Oz}} and the Politics of the Hebrew Language|author=Gal Koplewitz|work=The New Yorker|url=https://www.newyorker.com/books/page-turner/amos-oz-and-the-politics-of-the-hebrew-language|text=The written word, he often argued, could only ever be a low-fidelity reproduction of the fullness of '''being'''; any text was ultimately humbled by the reality that it sought to represent.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260918021615/https://www.newyorker.com/books/page-turner/amos-oz-and-the-politics-of-the-hebrew-language|archivedate=18 September 2026}}
# {{lb|en|philosophy}} That which has [[actuality]] ([[material]]ly or in [[concept]]).
# {{lb|en|philosophy}} One's basic nature, or the qualities thereof; [[essence]] or [[personality]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[abode]]; a [[cottage]].
#* {{RQ:Spectator|author=Richard Steele|VI|544|November 24, 1712|passage=It was a relief to dismiss them [Sir Roger's servants] into little '''beings''' within my manor.}}

